it is day two of the World Cup in Qatar  and while the games are well underway  it's what's happening off the pitch  that's making headlines the captains of  several European teams were planning on  playing with armband supporting  diversity and inclusion in a gesture  seen as a rebuke to katara's Human  Rights record but FIFA wouldn't have it  the governing body threatened players  with on-field punishment a risk they  knew would be too great for the athletes  to take  England Wales and Germany were three of  the seven European national teams who  wanted to set an example for tolerance  and inclusion with a rainbow-themed  armband and the statement one love FIFA  saw things differently while not  expressly Banning the armbands FIFA said  referees would sanction players who wore  them with a yellow card the Fifa Fifa  today banned a statement in favor of  diversity and human rights these are  values to which it commits itself in its  own statues this is more than  frustrating from our point of view  FIFA got their way and the evidence was  on the arm of England Captain Harry Kane  it appears that no European nation wants  to risk a yellow card to make a  political statement  and for more on this we can bring in  Mustafa qadri he's a specialist in human  rights and labor rights welcome to the  day Mr carty first it was supposed to be  a rainbow Armin then the teams bound to  pressure and decided on a one love  armband for diversity and inclusion now  I think they've settled on one reading  no discrimination why did FIFA raise the  stakes for players wanting to take a  stand at the World Cup  well I think to be honest firstly thank  you for having me on your show and for  raising these issues it's very important  I think frankly it's Death By A Thousand  Cuts what we've seen is backtracking by  FIFA and let's face it those behind them  which is the football associations the  teams and corporate sponsors uh towards  Qatar to effectively not address the  human rights issues in that country and  instead everyone else compromising for  them so really it's quite disappointing  but the other thing that's most  important is the signal it sends to the  lgbti community and did anyone facing  human rights issues not just in Qatar  not just in the region but globally you  have this very powerful prominent  players you know giving into this and of  course for FIFA itself that claims to be  a force for good respecting human rights  it sends the worst possible signal at  the very beginning of the tournament you  see you're disappointed but are you  surprised at all that the players aren't  willing to sacrifice their success in  order to make a statement  yeah it's a really good question so  obviously you know you could say it's  the World Cup players work very hard to  get to this very moment but then you  know it's such an interesting situation  we have where in the same game with the  Iranian team where players in that team  are probably risking their lives there's  probably a huge risk of you know serious  human rights abuse on them from the  Iranian authorities for being so I think  Brave and outspoken about the genuine  human rights crisis in Iran you  juxtapose that with the millionaire  footballs and the England team where  they don't seem to be able to go that  far of course you know I think it's  unfair to dump everything on the England  football team there's a deeper Point  here how did we get to this point how  did it we allow Qatar to be having this  world cup without adequate checks and  requirements on it to respect its human  rights responsibilities the thing that  viewers really need to understand is  that Qatar has ratified meaning it's  basically a codified into law some of  the most significant human rights  treaties in the world so that  International Covenant on civil and  political rights you know conventions  against discrimination so there's a hard  law requirements on Qatar and therefore  FIFA to respect human rights so yes  absolutely is a question that has to be  asked about these England football  players but there's a wider Community  here that's had 12 years to address this  issue and they clearly haven't and I  think that's the thing we really need to  be focusing on  are we in too deep right now is the  football community so broken that we  can't recover from something like this  or is there a way forward  it's a very good question so I think  when you work at human rights as I do  you're always inherently an optimistic  person and you know as Humanity you know  we've dealt with lots of huge challenges  um in human history including in Germany  so I do believe we can address this but  the only way we can address this is  through an honest discussion it starts  with things like the process through  which FIFA selects hosts for this  tournament having very robust human  rights requirements that also apply to  the teams to the corporate sponsors but  I think also what it means is being very  honest about what's happening in Qatar  right now you know while we were going  to air I heard that a journalist  reportedly in Qatar wearing a rainbow  Flag t-shirt has been told to take off  his T-shirt it seems like there's a very  serious issue there and of course the  work that we predominantly work on which  is the labor rights of the people who  built the stadiums is still there those  workers haven't gone anywhere they still  are owed compensation for unpaid wages  in some cases deaths were there a  competition to their relatives for  deaths and other issues so it's a very  concrete thing we're certainly going to  be pushing on those issues so that FIFA  the sponsors but also Qatar are held  accountable for human rights violation  that they're responsible for  yeah Qatar would certainly like for us  not to be talking about this right now  right it's day two of the World Cup and  hardly anybody is talking about the  matches themselves will the Emirate  succeed in pushing the controversies to  the background or is this going to  overshadow the entire world cup  yeah it's you know it's a question when  you hear when you say that to me I feel  very sad hearing that because I love my  football like everyone else does I want  to celebrate the football and that it's  in the Middle East you know I'm not from  the Middle East my family is it is  Muslim my background so it's something  that should be a time to celebrate but  you're quite right I think there's human  rights issues have have completely  overshadowed the tournament because  let's face it there's a very serious  issues there but it's simple things you  know I mean if for example the countries  were to allow their guests in the  country to wear things like rainbow  Flags allow armbands that are very soft  reflections of the need to respect the  rights of everyone that will go a long  way to making people feel like they're  doing the right thing so I think in many  ways Qatar itself is to blame for this  situation and of course so is FIFA you  know we obviously heard from Infantino  the president of FIFA on Saturday giving  a really bizarre and in some ways very  insulting speech the thing that people  really need to understand about that  speech is it's effectively him signaling  to the cutteries that he won't put any  pressure on them to address very real  human rights issues the thing to really  also emphasize is Qatar is not a free  Society it is a absolute monarchy the  opportunities for leverage on on things  like human rights and social reform are  very few and far between this is  probably the most unprecedented time to  have those kind of changes FIFA is one  of the few entities on the planet that  can actually influence how Qatar  responds to Human Rights so for them to  surrender the way that they have  absolutely the torment is being  overshadowed by these issues but there's  very good reason for that and on top of  that still even now there is time for  things to shift but frankly that window  is narrowing by the moment by the second  and this event will be over in a month  you already hinted at those workers that  will still be there after the World Cup  is over it's early days of course but I  want to get your take on what you  believe will be the legacy of this World  Cup  yeah it's a really good question I you  know I've worked on Qatar now for 10  years I've worked in human rights for  much longer than that but I think that  already even I'm surprised at how much  that the hard work that has been done by  good stakeholders in Qatar good people  in the cutlery system is being  squandered by the points that I just  raised I do worry that the Legacy will  be one that those changes will be  actually overshadowed but the failure to  really grab the nettle take those things  further I also worried to be honest with  you that if we're seeing the kind of  stories coming out from Qatar already  now arm bads being taken off people with  rainbow shirts being up being asked to  take them off on day two of the  tournament then you know are there going  to be other things that make it really a  fake on plea that this will be a  tournament that will be probably the  most you know um uh controversial in  history and that's that's really sad to  me it's not a manner of being  anti-cutter or a pro cutter it's there  are over 2 million workers in Qatar  there are thousands of people from the  lgbti community in Qatar there are women  who face all sorts of risks in that  country this was such a golden  opportunity in a part of the world which  is a very rich wonderful part of the  world I've traveled through it a lot  it's a wonderful part of the world but  it has such human rights issues in their  challenges and again the opportunities  to to really have some human rights wins  are so few and far between that yes it's  really quite sad that this could be  overshadowed by the situation but our  job is to keep monitoring that situation  to make sure for example workers get the  compensation of their own we're tracking  down as many workers as we can we're  working with other human rights  organizations and of course we're always  open to speak to FIFA and Qatar and the  stakeholders about what they can do to  address these things but I think we've  been very clear respect everyone's human  rights respect their dignity so that we  can actually enjoy the football and not  be banging on and on about human rights  during the middle of this huge  tournament  Mustafa Cadre specialists in human  rights and labor rights thank you so  much for your time it was a pleasure  speaking to you tonight  thank you so much
 

